# Two packages, each with a snapshot suite, asked for the way a person asks:
# `buri test`, with no target at all.
#
# **That invocation used to fail both of them**, with a sentence naming an
# environment variable. `buri test` puts several suites in one binary when their
# tags allow it, one process gets one snapshot directory, and a golden lives in
# its own *package's* `test/__snapshots__` — so a batch drawn from two packages
# had nowhere to put a picture and every `snapshot` in it aborted saying the
# directory had not been set. Asking for either suite by name worked, which is
# what made it a bug worth a case of its own: the failure was a property of the
# invocation rather than of the program.
#
# Both goldens are named `panel.png` on purpose. One directory per package is
# the claim, and two files with one name are how a run that lost track of which
# package it was in would show it: the second suite would compare against the
# first suite's picture and fail.
#
# The case is here rather than under `testing/` for `snapshots`' reason — the
# assertion is a picture — and it ends with the edit that makes it fire.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs.

doc: "a repository with two snapshot suites in it answers `buri test` with no target, each suite comparing against its own package's golden"

run {
  args: ["test"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "both.txt"
}

path { path: "lib/one/test/__snapshots__/panel.diff.png"  exists: false }
path { path: "lib/two/test/__snapshots__/panel.diff.png"  exists: false }

# The negative twin, and it is the one that says the two directories are still
# two: one package's tree changes, and only that package's suite fails.
edit {
  file: "lib/two/test/two.buri"
  replace: "ui.heading(2, .Const(\"two\"))"
  with: "ui.heading(2, .Const(\"three\"))"
}

run {
  args: ["test"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "one_of_them.txt"
}

path { path: "lib/two/test/__snapshots__/panel.diff.png"  exists: true }
path { path: "lib/one/test/__snapshots__/panel.diff.png"  exists: false }
